<main id="main">

<section class="sc-hero"><div class="sc-w sc-hero-g"><div class="sc-menu"><p class="sc-k">Ce soir &middot; Tonight</p><h1>A good sauce turns food into dinner.</h1><p class="sc-lead">Saucers is an independent home-cooking guide to sauces: quick pan sauces from what is left in the pan, proper gravy, and the five classic &ldquo;mother&rdquo; sauces. With tools that build a sauce, measure a roux and rescue one that goes wrong.</p><div class="sc-specials"><span>Pan sauce in 5 minutes</span><span>Lump-free gravy</span><span>The five mothers</span></div></div><figure class="sc-oval"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1775481494777-58a786c84303?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=640&q=75" alt="sauce being poured over a dish in a bowl" width="520" height="640" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></section>

<section class="sc-s sc-tiles"><div class="sc-w"><div class="sc-cen"><p class="sc-k">The family tree</p><h2>The five mother sauces</h2><p class="sc-lead">Learn these and you can make hundreds of sauces. Tap a mother to see her children.</p></div><div class="sc-tree"><div class="sc-mothers"><button type="button" class="sc-node" data-m="0"><b>B&eacute;chamel</b><span>Milk thickened with a white roux.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-node" data-m="1"><b>Velout&eacute;</b><span>Light stock thickened with a blond roux.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-node" data-m="2"><b>Espagnole</b><span>Brown stock with a brown roux and tomato.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-node" data-m="3"><b>Tomato</b><span>Tomatoes simmered with aromatics.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-node" data-m="4"><b>Hollandaise</b><span>Butter emulsified into egg yolks with lemon.</span></button></div><div class="sc-kids" id="mt-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="sc-s" id="builder"><div class="sc-w sc-bd"><div><p class="sc-k">Pan sauce builder</p><h2>Don&rsquo;t wash that pan</h2><p>The browned bits left after searing meat or fish are pure flavour. Choose what you cooked and how you want to finish it.</p><label for="pb-p">What you seared<select id="pb-p"><option value="0">Steak</option><option value="1">Chicken</option><option value="2">Pork chops</option><option value="3">Fish fillets</option><option value="4">Mushrooms</option></select></label><label for="pb-l">Liquid<select id="pb-l"><option value="0">Stock</option><option value="1">Cream and stock</option><option value="2">Lemon and stock</option><option value="3">Tomato and stock</option></select></label><label for="pb-f">Finish<select id="pb-f"><option value="0">Cold butter</option><option value="1">Mustard</option><option value="2">Fresh herbs</option><option value="3">Crushed peppercorns</option></select></label><figure class="sc-oval sc-oval-s"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792862283033-fc98c8cf77a8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="slices of seared steak with coarse salt on a tray" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"></figure></div><div class="sc-ticket" id="pb-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></section>

<section class="sc-s sc-cream" id="roux"><div class="sc-w sc-rx"><div><p class="sc-k">The roux calculator</p><h2>Equal parts, perfect body</h2><p>A roux is equal weights of fat and flour, cooked together, then whisked into liquid. The amount decides how thick the sauce will be.</p><label for="rx-v">Liquid (ml)<input id="rx-v" type="number" min="100" max="4000" step="50" value="500"></label><div class="sc-th" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Thickness"><label><input type="radio" name="th" value="0"><span>Thin<br><small>soup, gravy</small></span></label><label><input type="radio" name="th" value="1" checked><span>Medium<br><small>most sauces</small></span></label><label><input type="radio" name="th" value="2"><span>Thick<br><small>binding, gratins</small></span></label></div></div><div class="sc-rx-o"><div class="sc-scale" aria-hidden="true"><i id="rx-b"></i><i id="rx-f"></i></div><div id="rx-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="sc-s sc-tiles"><div class="sc-w sc-rs"><div class="sc-cen"><p class="sc-k">Sauce rescue</p><h2>When it goes wrong</h2></div><div class="sc-fxs"><button type="button" class="sc-fx" aria-pressed="false"><span class="sc-fx-f"><b>Lumpy</b><i>tap for the fix</i></span><span class="sc-fx-b">Whisk hard off the heat, or push it through a fine sieve. Next time add hot liquid to the roux gradually, whisking.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-fx" aria-pressed="false"><span class="sc-fx-f"><b>Too thin</b><i>tap for the fix</i></span><span class="sc-fx-b">Simmer to reduce, or whisk in a little cornflour mixed with cold water and boil for a minute.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-fx" aria-pressed="false"><span class="sc-fx-f"><b>Too thick</b><i>tap for the fix</i></span><span class="sc-fx-b">Whisk in warm stock, milk or water a spoonful at a time until it coats a spoon.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-fx" aria-pressed="false"><span class="sc-fx-f"><b>Split (butter or egg sauce)</b><i>tap for the fix</i></span><span class="sc-fx-b">Take it off the heat. Whisk in a spoonful of cold water, or start a new yolk and slowly whisk the split sauce into it.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-fx" aria-pressed="false"><span class="sc-fx-f"><b>Too salty</b><i>tap for the fix</i></span><span class="sc-fx-b">Add unsalted liquid or cream and a squeeze of lemon. Avoid reducing it further.</span></button><button type="button" class="sc-fx" aria-pressed="false"><span class="sc-fx-f"><b>Greasy</b><i>tap for the fix</i></span><span class="sc-fx-b">Spoon off fat from the surface, or blot with kitchen paper. Whisk in a splash of hot water to bring it back together.</span></button></div><figure class="sc-oval sc-oval-w"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1739242559784-d370950573bb?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&h=340&q=75" alt="a woman stirring a pot on a stove" width="760" height="340" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="sc-s"><div class="sc-w"><div class="sc-cen"><p class="sc-k">On the plate</p><h2>Plates and their sauces</h2></div><div class="sc-plates"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793571042054-e71f525e07fa?auto=format&fit=crop&w=340&h=340&q=75" alt="slices of seared fish with greens and a creamy sauce" width="340" height="340" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Fish</b>a creamy lemon sauce</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793195459235-84a4f8a24a1d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=340&h=340&q=75" alt="roast chicken with broccoli and sauce on a white plate" width="340" height="340" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Roast chicken</b>a pan gravy</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792046525586-d92a9dc7db1e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=340&h=340&q=75" alt="grilled lamb chops with herb butter, rice and yoghurt" width="340" height="340" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Lamb</b>herb butter and yoghurt</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794336457404-3df1623ee9d4?auto=format&fit=crop&w=340&h=340&q=75" alt="a bowl of creamy chicken curry with onion rings" width="340" height="340" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Curry</b>a sauce that is the dish</figcaption></figure></div></div></section>

<section class="sc-s sc-dark"><div class="sc-w"><div class="sc-cen"><p class="sc-k">Orders up</p><h2>From the kitchen</h2></div><div class="sc-rail"><a href="pan-sauce-guide.html" class="sc-tk"><small>Order #01 &middot; Table 4</small><b>The Pan Sauce Guide</b><em>Sear, deglaze, reduce, finish. Five minutes, every time.</em><i>Pick up &rarr;</i></a><a href="gravy-guide.html" class="sc-tk"><small>Order #02 &middot; Table 7</small><b>The Gravy Guide</b><em>Smooth, glossy gravy from drippings or stock.</em><i>Pick up &rarr;</i></a></div></div></section>

